.TH WPEXPORTJSON 1 "August 2026" "wpexportjson 1.8.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wpexportjson \- WordPress content export tool (REST API)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wpexportjson export
.RB [ \-u | \-\-url ]
.I URL
.RB [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wpexportjson
exports content from WordPress sites via the REST API. Supports multiple output formats including JSON, Markdown, a static\-site\-generator content source, and platform-specific formats for Shopify, Magento, and others.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Required Options"
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-url " " \fIURL\fR
WordPress site URL (required)
.SS "Output Options"
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Output directory (default: export/{domain}.{date})
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format: json, markdown, ssg, shopify, magento, wordpress, drupal, wix, squarespace, webflow, weebly, prestashop, ghost, strapi, contentful (default: json)
.IP
.B ssg
writes a drop\-in static\-site\-generator content source: pages nested to mirror their URL, posts under their category, single\-spelled front matter (\fBtitle\fR/\fBdescription\fR/\fBcategory\fR rather than WordPress's competing spellings), root\-relative addresses, and body HTML with entities decoded to UTF\-8, \fBalt\fR filled in from the media library and WordPress presentation classes dropped.
.TP
.BR \-\-zip
Create ZIP archive of export
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-files
Remove export files after creating ZIP (requires --zip)
.SS "Content Filters"
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-posts
Skip exporting blog posts
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-pages
Skip exporting pages
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-custom\-types
Skip the custom post types a theme or plugin registered (Services, Portfolio, Team, ...). They are exported by default: they are content the site published, and an export without them loses whole sections silently. WordPress internals, plugin bookkeeping types and a theme's saved layouts/templates are never exported.
.TP
.BR \-\-custom\-types " " \fISLUGS\fR
Export only these custom post types (comma\-separated slugs, e.g. \fBcpt_services,cpt_portfolio\fR)
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-products
Skip exporting WooCommerce products
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-users
Skip exporting users
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-tags
Skip exporting tags
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-comments
Skip exporting reader comments. They are exported by default into \fBcomments.json\fR, addressed by the page URL they belong to and sorted so a reply never precedes its parent; a site whose REST route is disabled or gated warns and carries on.
.TP
.BR \-\-path\-filter " " \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter posts/pages by URL path pattern (e.g., /fr/arts/)
.SS "Media Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-media
Download images and videos (default: true)
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-media
Disable media downloads
.TP
.BR \-\-relevant\-media\-only
Only download featured images and images embedded in content
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude\-media\-types " " \fITYPES\fR
Media types to skip (comma-separated: images,videos,audio,documents,archives)
.TP
.BR \-\-media\-path\-style " " \fISTYLE\fR
Form of rewritten media paths in exported content:
.B root
(default) emits root\-relative paths such as \fI/media/images/123_photo.jpg\fR, which resolve identically from any URL depth;
.B relative
emits \fImedia/images/123_photo.jpg\fR, correct only for content served from the site root (pre\-1.7.9 behaviour).
.TP
.BR \-\-report\-a11y
Write \fIa11y\-report.md\fR next to the export, flagging inline colours below the WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.3 minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 and images with no alt text (SC 1.1.1). The export itself is unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-\-link\-style " " \fISTYLE\fR
Form of the address fields \fBlink\fR, \fBcanonical_url\fR and \fBhreflangs\fR:
.B absolute
(default) keeps the source URL;
.B root
emits a root\-relative path such as \fI/2010/07/21/389/\fR, which preserves each URL (and its search ranking) when the site is rebuilt at the same paths on a new host. Only same\-host addresses are converted; an alternate or canonical on a different host is left untouched.
.SS "HTML Processing"
.TP
.BR \-\-flat\-html
Convert HTML to Markdown (Bricks Builder, Elementor support)
.TP
.BR \-\-basic\-html
Clean HTML to basic elements (tables, lists, links - for Shopify)
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve\-classes " " \fICLASSES\fR
CSS classes whose elements travel as the HTML they arrived as, rather than being converted (comma-separated, wildcards allowed: \fBklaviyo-form-*\fR, \fBtrx_addons_inline_*\fR, or \fB*\fR for every element carrying a class). Applies to \fBmarkdown\fR and \fBssg\fR as well as \fB\-\-flat\-html\fR and \fB\-\-basic\-html\fR: a heading's class is where themes of some families keep its colour, and a Markdown heading has nowhere to put it, so the migrated page renders the headline in the body colour instead.
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve\-ids " " \fIIDS\fR
Element IDs whose elements travel as the HTML they arrived as, rather than being converted (comma-separated, wildcards allowed).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep\-original\-urls
Preserve original WordPress URLs in content (don't convert to local paths). Without this flag the
.BR json ,
.B markdown
and
.B ssg
formats rewrite every reference to a downloaded attachment \- \fBsrc\fR, \fBhref\fR and \fBsrcset\fR alike \- matching scheme\- and host\-insensitively so historic \fIhttp://\fR, \fIwww\fR, former\-domain, protocol\-relative and root\-relative forms all resolve.
.SS "SEO Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-assisted\-crawl
Crawl URLs to extract SEO metadata: title, description, keywords, robots, the Open Graph and Twitter Card tags, article metadata, canonical, language and hreflang alternates. Every other meta tag is kept in a \fBmeta\fR map (see \fB\-\-extract\-meta\fR), \fBapplication/ld+json\fR blocks are preserved, and tracking identifiers (GA4, Universal Analytics, Google Tag Manager, Google Ads, Meta Pixel, Hotjar, Clarity, LinkedIn, TikTok) are collected site\-wide into \fImetadata.json\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-extract\-meta " " \fIPOLICY\fR
Which meta tags to keep beyond the named SEO fields:
.B all
(default) keeps every one \- plugins and themes put real information in tags nobody anticipated, and a generator can ignore a key it does not recognise but cannot recover one the export dropped;
.B none
keeps only the named fields; or a comma\-separated allow\-list such as \fBtwitter:card,article:author\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-crawl\-content
Take the rendered page wherever the stored body is not the page. Two cases: a body the REST API serves empty, and a body that is a page builder's scaffolding \- several kilobytes of nested wrappers with a headline somewhere inside, which renders to nothing without the plugin that builds it. The second is judged by what the body amounts to rather than by its length: an ordinary page carries hundreds of characters of text per container element and a shell carries a handful, and a known builder's class prefix (\fBkc-elm\fR, \fBvc_row\fR, \fBet_pb_\fR, \fBelementor-\fR, \fBfl-builder\fR, \fBbrxe-\fR, \fBoxy-\fR) raises the threshold rather than being the whole rule. The run states how many of each it found.
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude\-tags " " \fITAGS\fR
SEO tags to exclude (comma-separated: title,meta:description,og:title,canonical,lang,hreflangs)
.TP
.BR \-\-skip\-empty\-content
Skip posts/pages with empty content from export
.SS "Cache Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-cache
Enable caching of API responses and crawl data
.TP
.BR \-\-cache\-ttl " " \fIDURATION\fR
Cache TTL (e.g., 24h, 1h, 0 for unlimited) (default: 24h)
.TP
.BR \-\-cache\-dir " " \fIPATH\fR
Cache directory (default: ~/.wpexporter/cache)
.TP
.BR \-\-cache\-clear
Clear cache before export
.SS "Authentication"
.TP
.BR \-\-auth\-user " " \fIUSERNAME\fR
Username for Basic Auth (prompts for password if --auth-pass not provided)
.TP
.BR \-\-auth\-pass " " \fIPASSWORD\fR
Password for Basic Auth
.TP
.BR \-\-auth\-token " " \fITOKEN\fR
Bearer token for authentication
.SS "Performance Options"
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrent " " \fINUM\fR
Concurrent downloads (default: 5)
.TP
.BR \-\-rate\-limit " " \fIMS\fR
Delay between API requests in milliseconds (prevents server rate limiting)
.TP
.BR \-\-frontmatter\-style " " \fISTYLE\fR
Form of the front-matter values that are not flat \- the \fBmeta\fR map and the \fBhreflangs\fR list. \fInested\fR (default) writes YAML structure, which is what a generator reading the files wants. \fIflat\fR writes each as a single JSON string, so it survives a store whose metadata model is key to list of strings and can be decoded back into the same structure; \fBjson_ld\fR already travels as text and is unchanged either way.
.TP
.BR \-\-from\-sitemap
Recover posts from the site's main feed when the REST API serves none \- a site whose \fI/wp/v2/posts\fR answers 5xx for every request still publishes its feed. Recovered records carry title, address, date, author and body, and no IDs, taxonomy terms or featured images; \fBstats.recovered_posts\fR states how many there were. The feed lists recent items only, so this never replaces or merges with a collection the API did serve. A WordPress older than 4.7 has no content API at all, so the run turns this on for itself there rather than handing back an empty export.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-inventory\-check
Skip the completeness check. After the export the site's own sitemap and main feed are read \- one or two requests \- and every address they list that the export does not carry is reported, grouped by path, and recorded in \fBmetadata.json\fR under \fBstats.uncovered\fR. Archive views a generator rebuilds itself (\fI/tag/\fR, \fI/category/\fR, date archives) are not counted. A site that publishes neither document says so and the export is unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit " " \fIN\fR
Export at most \fIN\fR documents in total, newest first (default: no limit). The walk stops when the budget is spent rather than fetching everything and discarding the rest, so a preview of five pages costs one request instead of five hundred records. Media is limited to what the exported documents reference, and the summary states the truncation as \fBPosts: 5 (limited from 75)\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit\-per\-type " " \fISPEC\fR
At most \fIN\fR of each kind, or \fIkind=N\fR pairs, or both: \fB5\fR, \fBposts=5,media=10\fR, \fB5,media=10\fR. A kind is a collection name (\fBposts\fR, \fBpages\fR, \fBmedia\fR, \fBproducts\fR) or a custom type's slug, since there can be any number of those. Combines with \fB\-\-limit\fR, whichever is smaller.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit\-posts ", " \-\-limit\-pages ", " \-\-limit\-media ", " \-\-limit\-products " " \fIN\fR
Shortcuts for the kinds a preview is usually shaped around. Where one names a kind \fB\-\-limit\-per\-type\fR also names, the dedicated flag wins and the run says which value it used \- a silent choice between two numbers the operator asked for is worse than either.
.TP
.BR \-\-user\-agent " " \fISTRING\fR
The User\-Agent to send (default: \fIWordPress\-Export\-JSON/1.0\fR). Bot protection matches on the default, so a browser's string is the remedy that most often works against a \fB403\fR served by a wall rather than by WordPress. A refusal that carries Cloudflare's marks is named as such in the report, with the remedies that apply to it.
.TP
.BR \-\-retries " " \fINUM\fR
Attempts for a request the site answers with 5xx or 429, or drops (default: 3). Backoff is exponential with jitter and honours \fBRetry\-After\fR. A page of results that still will not come is reported as an incomplete collection and the export continues with what it has.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
HTTP request timeout in seconds (default: 30)
.TP
.BR \-\-resume
Resume from checkpoint if previous export was interrupted
.SS "Other Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-brute\-force
Enable brute force ID discovery
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-id " " \fINUM\fR
Maximum ID for brute force (default: 10000)
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-ids
Exclude numeric IDs from frontmatter
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress all output, only return exit code
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Configuration file path
.SH "THE SITE'S REST API"
WordPress serves its REST API two ways: the pretty \fI/wp\-json/wp/v2/\&...\fR, and \fI/?rest_route=/wp/v2/\&...\fR, which works with no permalink structure at all. Sites serving only the second are common enough to matter \- permalinks set to plain, or a security plugin hiding \fI/wp\-json/\fR \- and they are read without being asked about.
.PP
The discovery is lazy, because the pretty spelling is what nearly every site answers to: it is tried first, nothing is probed until a request actually comes back \fB404\fR, and a site whose API works spends no extra request at all. The site that needs the fallback pays one. Which spelling was used is stated in the report and in \fBstats.notices\fR in \fBmetadata.json\fR, since the address there is not the one a reader would try by hand.
.PP
A WordPress older than \fB4.7\fR has neither spelling: the \fBwp/v2\fR content routes arrived in that release, and an older install answers \fBrest_no_route\fR to both. There is nothing to fall back to, so the run states it once, records it in \fBstats.notices\fR, and reads what the site still publishes in its feed \- an empty export that looks like an empty site is the failure this replaces.
.SH "THE WPEXPORTER COMMAND"
The toolkit installs four binaries. Three are the individual tools:
.BR wpexportjson " (REST API, this page), " wpxmlrpc " (XML-RPC) and " wpmcp " (MCP server)."
The fourth,
.BR wpexporter ,
is an umbrella over all three, so one command covers the toolkit:
.PP
.RS
wpexporter export  \(em same as \fBwpexportjson export\fR
.br
wpexporter xmlrpc  \(em same as \fBwpxmlrpc\fR
.br
wpexporter mcp     \(em same as \fBwpmcp\fR
.RE
.PP
Every flag on this page applies to
.B wpexporter export
unchanged.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Basic Export"
Export a WordPress site to JSON:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com
.RE
.SS "Markdown Export with SEO"
Export to Markdown with SEO metadata:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com -f markdown --assisted-crawl
.RE
.SS "Shopify Export with Preserve"
Export for Shopify, preserving Klaviyo forms:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com -f shopify --basic-html \\
  --preserve-classes "klaviyo-form-*"
.RE
.SS "Keeping a Theme's Generated Classes"
Migrate to Markdown while keeping the elements the theme colours through a generated class:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com -f markdown \\
  --preserve-classes "trx_addons_inline_*"
.RE
.SS "Static Site Generator Source"
Export a drop-in content source and check it for accessibility problems before publishing:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com -f ssg --assisted-crawl --report-a11y
.RE
.SS "Cached Export"
Use caching for faster repeated exports:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com --cache --cache-ttl 24h
.RE
.SS "Filtered Export"
Export only French art pages:
.PP
.RS
wpexportjson export --url https://example.com --path-filter "/fr/arts/"
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_URL
WordPress site URL
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_OUTPUT
Output directory
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_FORMAT
Output format
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_CACHE
Enable caching (true/false)
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_CACHE_TTL
Cache TTL
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_CACHE_DIR
Cache directory
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_PRESERVE_CLASSES
CSS classes to preserve (comma-separated)
.TP
.B WPEXPORT_PRESERVE_IDS
Element IDs to preserve (comma-separated)
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./config.yaml
Local configuration file
.TP
.I ~/.wpexportjson/config.yaml
User configuration file
.TP
.I /etc/wpexportjson/config.yaml
System configuration file
.TP
.I ~/.wpexporter/cache/
Default cache directory
.TP
.I .wpexport_checkpoint.json
Checkpoint file for resume functionality
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS "Homebrew (macOS / Linux)"
.nf
brew install tradik/tap/wpexporter
.fi
.SS "Snap (Linux)"
.nf
sudo snap install wpexporter
.fi
.SS "From Source"
.nf
git clone https://github.com/tradik/wpexporter.git
cd wpexporter
make build
.fi
